<Guide slug="pos-when-the-internet-goes-off" waMessage={WA_MESSAGES.guideOffline} ctaTitle="Pull the cable out and watch it keep selling.">
  <p>
    In Kenya the internet goes off. So does the power. Not every day, but always at the worst moment: the Friday lunch
    rush, the end-of-month queue at the minimart, the evening when every matatu stops for fuel. When it happens, a
    point of sale that needs the internet to sell is not a point of sale. It is a screen that says "Please wait".
  </p>
  <p>
    Before you buy any system, it is worth knowing exactly what it does when the line drops. Here is what a point of
    sale should keep doing, what can safely wait, and what to ask.
  </p>

  <h2>What must keep working</h2>
  <p>These are the things a customer would notice. None of them should depend on the internet:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Selling.</strong> Adding items, taking payment, printing the receipt.</li>
    <li><strong>Signing in.</strong> A cashier who comes on shift during an outage must still be able to start work with their own PIN, not borrow someone else's.</li>
    <li><strong>The barcode scanner and the scale.</strong> At a shop or a minimart, scanning and weighing are the queue.</li>
    <li><strong>The kitchen.</strong> At a restaurant or café, orders must still reach the kitchen printer or screen.</li>
    <li><strong>The pumps.</strong> At a petrol station, the attendant must still be able to sell fuel by amount or by litres, on the right pump.</li>
    <li><strong>Every point of sale in the branch, in step.</strong> If you have two counters, both must see the same open orders and the same stock, even with no internet.</li>
  </ul>

  <figure>
    <Monitor label="The real ZapTill point of sale at a demo petrol station">
      <Shot src="/screens/till-pumps.webp" width={1280} height={800}
        alt="The real ZapTill point of sale at a demo petrol station: five pumps with their grade and price per litre, and a sale of diesel with engine oil and water from the shop." />
    </Monitor>
    <figcaption>The pumps are kept on the point of sale itself, so the forecourt keeps selling with no internet. A demo business.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What can wait</h2>
  <p>Some things genuinely need the internet. That is fine, as long as they catch up by themselves:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Seeing your numbers from somewhere else, on your phone or laptop.</li>
    <li>The daily summary email.</li>
    <li>Sales reaching the cloud, and branches seeing each other.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The test is simple: when the line comes back, does everything arrive on its own, or does someone have to type the
    afternoon's sales in again? Retyping is where mistakes and missing money come from.
  </p>

  <h2>And the power?</h2>
  <p>
    Be honest with yourself here. A point of sale on a computer needs electricity, like any computer. No software keeps
    a PC running through a KPLC cut. What does is a UPS: a battery box between the wall and the computer, the receipt
    printer and the router. A small one keeps the counter going through a short cut and gives you time to close
    properly in a long one. If a system claims to "work through power cuts" without mentioning a UPS or a battery, ask
    how.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-zt">
    <p class="guide-zt__h">How ZapTill does it</p>
    <p>
      The ZapTill desktop point of sale keeps selling and printing with no internet: the scanner, the kitchen tickets
      and the pumps. Staff still sign in with their PIN. A branch server in the shop keeps every point of sale in the
      branch in step, and when the internet comes back everything catches up by itself; nobody types anything in again.
      A UPS is on our recommended hardware list for every branch.
    </p>
    <p>
      Honestly: the web dashboard and the daily email need the internet, and so does parking, which runs on the web
      point of sale.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Questions to ask before you buy</h2>
  <p>Ask any supplier these, and ask them to show you, not tell you:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>"Unplug the internet now. Can I still sell, print and sign in a new cashier?"</li>
    <li>"If I have two points of sale, do they still share the same orders and stock with no internet?"</li>
    <li>"Do kitchen tickets, the scanner and the pumps still work?"</li>
    <li>"When the line is back, what happens to the sales? Does anyone retype anything?"</li>
    <li>"What happens in a power cut, and what hardware do you recommend for it?"</li>
    <li>"Which parts need the internet, honestly?"</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A good answer to the last question is a short list, said without hesitation. Every system needs the internet for
    something. The point is knowing what, before the Friday lunch rush finds out for you.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-note">
    <p>
      <strong>What about M-Pesa?</strong> The customer pays from their own phone, on Safaricom's network, not your shop's
      internet. The cashier records the sale as M-Pesa as usual. Do check the payment has arrived on the business phone
      before handing over the goods, internet or not.
    </p>
  </div>
</Guide>
